  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 256 AM EDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nNo major changes. An Omega-ish block, anchored by upper lows off\nthe east coast, and moving onto the west coast, will promote\nabove-normal heights for the central Conus for quite some time.\nThere is an actual shortwave of note in the near term, crossing\nthe northern lakes Wed afternoon. Its eventual fate is be\nsheared out across New England, before getting absorbed into\nbroader east coast troffing.\n\nThe shortwave Wed afternoon does provide a reasonable shot of\nDPVA forcing in northern areas, and arrives at a favorable time\nof day. But it has very little moisture to work with. It would\nnot be shocking to see the Straits area and the eastern UP see \na stray shower Wed afternoon/early evening. But that chances is \n10 percent or less, and will not be included in the current \nforecast. We will see increased cloud cover moving thru Wed, \nespecially again in the north. But temps will still be as warm \nor warmer than today, thanks to a zone of warm advection ahead \nof a weakening cold front. The airmass behind that front will \ntake a few degrees off of max temps for the rest of the week.\n\nThe upper pattern becomes a bit more mobile this weekend. But\nthe upper trof pushing east across the western states only\nserves to amplify the ridging over the Great Lakes this weekend.\nExpect temps to trend a bit warmer again moving thru the\nweekend. The increasingly mobile/energetic northern stream\nbecomes harder to get a firm grasp of next week, but plenty of\nguidance brings a stronger cold front into the region, and\nsharply colder wx by Tuesday (even with unimpressive pops).",
